# [DIEM](../wg/diem.html)

## Summary

The Digital Emblems (DIEM) working group met at IETF 120. The session focused on confirming the consensus baseline of the requirements draft, reviewing three architectural proposals, discussing DNS and cryptographic design constraints, and establishing a unified direction for the working group's architecture document.

Key outcomes included confirming that [draft-ietf-diem-requirements](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-diem-requirements/) (-05) reflects working group consensus to inform architectural development, agreeing to build a unified architecture draft based on a proposed skeleton structure, and forming a design team to produce the initial draft.

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group & Requirements Status
- Working group co-chair Dennis Jackson introduced himself, stepping in during Sarah Tarrant's temporary absence alongside co-chair Rohan Mahy.
- Felix Linker presented [Update - Use Cases and Requirements Document](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-diem-update-use-cases-and-requirements-document-00), covering changes between version -03 (which underwent Working Group Last Call) and version -05 of [draft-ietf-diem-requirements](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-diem-requirements/).
- Changes in -05 were editing and terminology consolidations (e.g., standardizing on "emblem profiles", "validate", and "validator") without adding or deleting normative requirements. Expanded details were added for the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) and UN Peacekeepers use cases.
- The chairs confirmed that draft-ietf-diem-requirements-05 represents working group consensus and will serve as the requirement baseline to inform architecture development.

### Architecture Proposals and Comparison
Three presentations outlined different facets of DIEM architecture:

1. **Alex Rosenberg**: [Rosenberg-diem-overview](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-diem-rosenberg-diem-overview-00)
   - Proposed delivering a dictionary of typed metadata via DNS secured by DNSSEC using a new Resource Record (RR) type.
   - Advocated CBOR encoding for typed data structures, zone delegations (e.g., `_dm`), and reusable metadata components ("facets").
   - Recommended handling selective disclosure via application-layer encryption (COSE/CBOR) rather than DNS-level access control or client identification.

2. **Rahel**: [Proposed Architecture for Digital Emblems](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-diem-proposed-architecture-for-digital-emblems-00)
   - Highlighted DNSSEC-based metadata organization, key-value taxonomy, and scoped/underscored naming conventions.
   - Raised questions around selective disclosure and validation flows across zone boundaries.

3. **Felix Linker**: [Architecture Document Considerations](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-diem-architecture-document-considerations-01)
   - Discussed positioning the architecture document at an intermediate specificity level between abstract principles and full protocol specifications.
   - Proposed a unified architecture skeleton (Slide 13) comprising:
     1. Shared domain model (issuers, authorizers, validators).
     2. Core architectural components (emblem formats, authorization, validation, distribution).
     3. Profiles/vignettes mapping specific use cases to core components.
     4. Considerations for future extensions.

### Technical Discussion: DNS Constraints, Encryption, and Scope
- **DNS Limits and Pointers**: Lars Liman, Jim Reid, and Martin Thomson noted that DNS is unsuitable for large payloads. Large metadata payloads should be stored externally with DNS providing pointers (e.g., pinned via DANE or CBOR Web Tokens).
- **Selective Disclosure in DNS**: Martin Thomson, Jim Reid, Chris Lemons, and Orie Steele pointed out that providing differential responses or authenticating clients directly within DNS queries is unviable due to caching recursive resolvers. Selective disclosure should occur out-of-band or via encrypted fields.
- **Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC)**: Jim Reid and Alex Rosenberg noted PQC key and signature sizes in DNSSEC emphasize the need to keep on-chain DNS records compact.
- **Authentication & Threat Models**: Robert Carolina questioned the necessity of cryptographic authentication compared to physical emblem legal precedents under International Humanitarian Law (IHL). Felix Linker, Rohan Mahy, and Dennis Jackson explained that authentication provides technical accountability against misuse and that while some emblem profiles may be unauthenticated, the general architecture must support optional authorization mechanisms.

### External Updates
- Samit Kuna (ICRC) provided an update on external DIEM and IHL digital emblem testing:
  - Phase 2 launch at CERN focused on technical testing with National Red Cross/Red Crescent societies (e.g., Australia, UK, Belgium, Bolivia, Costa Rica, Qatar, Nigeria) and NATO cyber defense exercises.
  - Recognition at the World Internet Conference and ongoing discussions at the ITU Plenipotentiary and International Committee of Military Medicine.
- Jim Reid suggested sharing external SDO and project updates regularly on the DIEM mailing list.

## Decisions and Action Items

### Session Polls
The chairs ran an official poll regarding adopting the proposed architecture skeleton:
- **Question**: "Do we want to form an architecture based on the skeleton described in the slide 13?"
- **Result**: yes: 11, no: 1, no_opinion: 6 (total: 36)

### Key Decisions
- The working group agreed to construct a unified architecture document using the skeleton structure proposed on Slide 13 of [Architecture Document Considerations](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-diem-architecture-document-considerations-01).
- [draft-ietf-diem-requirements](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-diem-requirements/) (-05) was confirmed as the consensus baseline for informing architectural work.

## Next Steps
- A design team consisting of Felix Linker, Rahel, and Alex Rosenberg was formed to author the merged architecture draft.
- The chairs will establish synchronous and asynchronous coordination channels for the design team to advance the architecture draft prior to the next IETF meeting.